.TH EGRAPH 1 2026-09-29 "egraph @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
egraph \- query the dependency graph of the installed packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B egraph
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command " [" command-options ] " [" arguments ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B egraph
keeps the dependency graph of a Gentoo system in a store file and answers questions about it in
milliseconds: what depends on a package, why it is installed, who links a soname, what is broken,
what
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove and what
.B emerge \-u
would merge.
.PP
The store is built by
.BR egraph-build (1),
which evaluates the installed packages through portage.
Every command first checks that the store still describes the system and brings it up to date
when it does not, unless
.B \-\-no\-refresh
says otherwise.
The answers are held to portage's own on generated systems and on a live one.
.PP
Without a command,
.B egraph
opens its interactive interface on a terminal (the same as
.BR "egraph tui" ),
and reads commands one per line otherwise (the same as
.BR "egraph shell" ).
.PP
Packages are named by installed cpv
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl-3.5.0 )
or cp
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl ,
every installed version); atoms take portage's syntax.
.PP
Run as
.BI egraph\- command
(a link to
.BR egraph ),
it runs that command:
.B egraph\-orphans
is
.BR "egraph orphans" ,
the global options taken among its own.
.SH OPTIONS
These come before the command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options, or a command's, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The root whose installed packages to query (default
.BR $ROOT ,
else
.IR / ).
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-root " dir"
The root of the portage configuration to evaluate them with (default
.BR $PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-eprefix " dir"
The offset prefix of a prefix installation (default
.BR $PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX ).
.TP
.BI \-\-store " file"
The store to read (default
.BR $EGRAPH_STORE ,
else the system store; see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI \-\-replace\-slots " file"
The list of packages whose old slots a plan replaces (default
.BR $EGRAPH_REPLACE_SLOTS ,
else
.I etc/egraph/replace\-slots
under the configuration root; see
.BR FILES );
.I /dev/null
keeps every slot, as emerge.
.TP
.BI \-\-builder " command"
The
.B egraph-build
that refreshes the store (default
.BR $EGRAPH_BUILD ,
else the one next to
.BR egraph ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-\-emerge " command"
The
.B emerge
that
.B \-\-verify
and the actions run (default
.BR $EGRAPH_EMERGE ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-\-dispatch\-conf " command"
The
.B dispatch\-conf
an action offers when configuration updates are waiting (default
.BR $EGRAPH_DISPATCH_CONF ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
It takes the roots from
.BR ROOT ", " PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT " and " PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX .
.TP
.BI \-\-emaint " command"
The
.B emaint
that
.B sync
runs (default
.BR $EGRAPH_EMAINT ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ),
with the roots in the environment as for
.BR dispatch\-conf .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-refresh
Answer from a stale store instead of bringing it up to date.
.TP
.BR \-\-layout " auto|human|lines"
Results for people, or as tab-separated lines for scripts (default
.BR $EGRAPH_LAYOUT ,
else
.BR auto :
for people on a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-\-log " auto|journal|file|both|none"
Where runs that change the system are logged (default
.BR $EGRAPH_LOG ,
else
.BR auto :
the systemd journal when systemd runs and egraph was built with it, else the file). The
journal's entries carry
.B SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER=egraph
and fields under
.BR EGRAPH_ ,
as
.B journalctl \-t egraph EGRAPH_EVENT=failed
finds them; the file holds one JSON object a line. Neither is emerge's log.
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-file " file"
The log file (default
.BR $EGRAPH_LOG_FILE ,
else
.IR ${EPREFIX}/var/log/egraph.log ).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Colour the layout for people (default
.BR auto :
on a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set).
.TP
.BR \-\-glyphs " nerd|unicode|ascii"
Icons in the layout for people: a Nerd Font's, plain Unicode, or ASCII (default
.BR $EGRAPH_GLYPHS ,
else
.B nerd
in a UTF-8 locale and
.B ascii
otherwise).
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that read dependencies take
.BR \-\-dynamic\-deps " y|n" :
read an installed package's dependencies from its ebuild when the same version is still in its
repository, as emerge's option of the name does (default
.BR y ).
Those that follow what keeps a package take
.BR \-\-with\-bdeps " y|n" :
whether build-time dependencies keep packages, as emerge's option does (default
.BR y ).
.SS deps \fIpackage\fR...
What installed packages depend on, with the atom and the kinds of dependency for each.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would add with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS rdeps \fIpackage\fR...
What depends on installed packages.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would depend on them with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS why \fIatom\fR
The shortest chain of dependencies from a root set
.RI ( @selected ", " @system ", " @profile )
that keeps each installed package the atom matches, as depclean finds it.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS match \fIatom\fR...
The installed packages each atom matches.
.TP
.B \-\-candidates
Match the installed cps' ebuilds instead
.RI ( cpv::repo ),
with the USE each would be built with now, masked ones included.
.TP
.B \-\-config
Read the atoms as configuration files such as
.I package.use
do, wildcards and all
.RI ( */* ", " dev-lang/rust* ),
and match the ebuilds as
.B \-\-candidates
does.
Each ebuild's atoms are listed in the order portage applies their entries, least specific
first, so the last one wins.
.SS use \fIpackage\fR [\fIflag\fR]
Each USE flag's state for the ebuilds that
.I package
matches, an atom as configuration files take it, wildcards and all
.RI ( */* " for every ebuild the stores hold), and where it was last set:"
.IR file : line ,
or the layer of a package's own
.RI ( pkginternal " for its IUSE defaults, " features " for FEATURES=test, " arch ).
Flags are shown as emerge shows them: off with a
.BR \- ,
masked or forced in parentheses.
The state is stacked from the configuration as portage stacks it, profile by profile, then
.IR make.conf ,
.I package.use
and the environment, and holds to the USE each ebuild would be built with now.
.SS soname \fIsoname\fR
The installed packages that need a soname
.RI ( libssl.so.3 ).
.TP
.B \-\-providers
The packages providing it instead.
.SS broken
Installed dependencies that nothing installed satisfies.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS search \fIkey\fR...
The packages in the repositories, and those installed, whose names match each
.IR key ,
as
.B emerge \-\-search
finds them: a
.I key
matches a name anywhere, ignoring case, or one alike (see
.BR \-\-fuzzy\-search );
it is a regular expression after a
.BR % ,
or when it looks like one; with a
.B /
in it, or after a
.BR @ ,
it matches the whole category/package name.
Each package shows its best visible version (or its best, marked masked), the latest installed,
and its ebuild's homepage, license and description.
In lines, tab-separated: the key, the package, the version,
.B visible
or
.BR masked ,
the latest installed version (empty when none), the homepage, the license and the description.
Unlike emerge, it shows no download sizes and matches no sets.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-searchdesc
Match each package's description too, as emerge picks it.
.TP
.BR \-\-fuzzy\-search " y|n"
Also match names alike, by difflib's similarity (default
.BR y ).
.TP
.BI \-\-search\-similarity " n"
How alike, in percent, a fuzzy match must be (default 80).
.TP
.BR \-\-regex\-search\-auto " y|n"
Take a key that looks like a regular expression for one (default
.BR y ).
.SS versions \fR[\fIpackage\fR ...]
Every version in the repositories of each
.I package
(an atom, or a name without its category for every category's), or of every package without
any, with its slot, its repository and, for a masked one, why, as
.B emerge
words it
.RB ( package.mask ,
.BR "~amd64 keyword" ,
.BR "EULA license(s)" ).
egraph decides visibility itself, from the repository index; portage's decisions are the tests'
reference.
In lines,
.IR cpv :: repo ,
the slot (with its sub-slot when that differs), and
.B visible
or
.B masked
followed by the reasons, tab-separated.
.SS blockers \fR[\fIpackage\fR ...]
Blockers
.RB ( ! atom
and
.BR !! atom)
in the installed packages' dependencies, each with the installed packages it blocks: of the
packages given, every blocker they hold and every blocker holding them back; without packages,
the blockers that block something installed.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS orphans
The installed packages
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS updates
The installed packages
.B emerge \-u
would replace or rebuild, and the new packages it would pull in, each with the USE it would be
built with as
.B emerge \-v
shows it; a new package beside installed ones in other slots of its name is a new slot
.RB ( new\-slot ,
emerge's NS), with those installed versions.
With
.BR \-D ,
a dependency naming no slot goes to its best version as emerge's does, into a new slot beside
the installed one when that is where the best version is.
Then the installed packages it would uninstall for a blocker between them and a merge,
and the old slots it replaces: those
.I replace\-slots
lists, which a world atom naming no slot leaves for a new one, uninstalled once the new slot
merges unless something left installed still needs them (emerge keeps them for
.BR "emerge \-\-depclean" ).
On the running system's root, a slot holding the running kernel's sources (where its modules'
.I build
link points, as
.B egraph\-build \-\-kernel\-sources
reads the packages' contents) is never replaced, and every slot is kept when that cannot be told;
and the blockers it could not resolve, for which it refuses the whole plan (exit status 6). On
the running system's root, emerge only resolves a strong blocker
.RB ( !! atom)
by a replacement.
Last, the installed packages emerge warns are masked: those the plan keeps that are not visible
as installed
.RI ( package.mask ,
an unaccepted license, invalid metadata or an unsupported EAPI) and that its graph holds, or
that a license masks, each with the reasons emerge gives and, for
.IR package.mask ,
the file and the comment above the entry, as emerge shows them (a comment once).
A merge whose dependency nothing satisfies, or an ebuild with invalid metadata, is passed over for
another version, as emerge backtracks; when what emerge must merge has none left, the plan ends
with the dependencies no visible version matches, and emerge refuses it too.
So it does a version emerge would select whose REQUIRED_USE its USE leaves unsatisfied, even one
it then passes over: the plan ends with the constraints left unmet, which a
.I package.use
change must meet. A USE dependency no version meets as it would be built takes the USE change
emerge's autounmask asks for, on the best version that can take it: the plan is made with it
and ends with the
.I package.use
lines, each with what requires it, and emerge refuses it until they are written. Run from a
terminal, with the human layout,
.BR plan " and " updates
offer to write them: on yes they are appended to
.I package.use
under the configuration root (to
.I package.use/zz-autounmask
when that is a directory), and the plan is made again on stores refreshed for them.
.B \-\-verify
compares the USE changes emerge asks for, and only them: its merge list then is made before
some of them.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
Also the updates that installed dependents hold back, which of their atoms do, and the commands
that would get past them.
Without it, only the updates held back by a dependency nothing satisfies are listed (the
target's own, or one of what it would pull in: an error in a repository), each with that
dependency; the summary counts the rest.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for, each followed by its kinds:
.B b
build (DEPEND, BDEPEND),
.B i
install (IDEPEND),
.B r
run (RDEPEND),
.B p
post (PDEPEND),
.B l
emerge's implicit wait on a libc it merges, and
.B t
through installed packages it depends on.
A place after its own is a wait the order breaks.
An uninstall lists the places of the merges whose blockers need it gone: as emerge does, it
goes once they are merged, both installed meanwhile.
The human layout lists only the earlier places waited for by
.BR b ,
.BR i ,
.B r
or
.BR p .
.TP
.B \-\-tree
Each merge under the root set and the packages it comes from.
.TP
.B \-\-world
Only the packages the root sets keep, as
.B emerge \-u @world
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
Every package in scope, not only the arguments and what their merges need, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does; with
.BR \-\-world ,
what the versions the root sets move to depend on, through the one alternative of each
.B ||
emerge would take.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes for changed USE or IUSE.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes for changed USE.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges or uninstalls, a
different kind of merge, a new package's USE, or a blocker only one of them cannot resolve. The
differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and make the exit status 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume\-list " file"
Also write the updates to
.I file
as the merge list
.B emerge \-\-resume
reads (the
.I resume
entry of portage's
.IR mtimedb ,
as JSON), with
.B \-\-oneshot
among its options. emerge resolves the blockers and orders the merges again when it resumes;
the list carries no uninstalls. A plan emerge would refuse is not written, nor a request for
nothing (an empty set).
.TP
.BI \-\-requests " file"
Also write the updates to
.I file
as the requests
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-worker" " (" egraph\-build (1))
takes, a JSON object a line, in the order one worker would carry them out: each merge with the
installed packages it blocks or that block it then, and each uninstall straight after the merges
it waits for, as
.B emerge
runs them. Nothing joins the world file, as with
.BR \-\-oneshot .
Written as
.B \-\-resume\-list
is.
.SS plan \fItarget\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-pretend
would merge for the targets, in the lines
.B updates
prints: atoms (a name without its category takes the one the repositories know it in) and the sets
.BR @world ", " @selected ", " @system ", " @profile ", " @installed
and
.BR @preserved\-rebuild ,
which
.B egraph\-build \-\-notices
loads as emerge does (as root: only root and the portage group may read the registry of
preserved libraries).
A package the request names is merged even when installed, as plain
.B emerge
does. A package only the repositories know is evaluated first by
.BR egraph\-build " \-\-evaluate"
(not with
.BR \-\-no\-refresh ),
and stays in the store until
.BR "egraph rebuild" .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Update each installed slot a target matches instead, and add its best version's slot, as
.B emerge \-u
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
With
.BR \-u ,
every package the targets lead to through the versions they move to, and the one alternative
of each
.B ||
emerge would take, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
Skip a target something installed matches, as
.B emerge \-\-noreplace
does.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes.
.TP
.B \-\-held
As for
.BR updates .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for, each followed by its kinds:
.B b
build (DEPEND, BDEPEND),
.B i
install (IDEPEND),
.B r
run (RDEPEND),
.B p
post (PDEPEND),
.B l
emerge's implicit wait on a libc it merges, and
.B t
through installed packages it depends on.
A place after its own is a wait the order breaks.
An uninstall lists the places of the merges whose blockers need it gone: as emerge does, it
goes once they are merged, both installed meanwhile.
The human layout lists only the earlier places waited for by
.BR b ,
.BR i ,
.B r
or
.BR p .
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges, a different kind of
merge, or a new package's USE. The differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and
make the exit status 5.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume\-list " file"
As for
.BR updates ,
the targets among the list's favorites, which emerge selects once they are merged.
.TP
.BI \-\-requests " file"
As for
.BR updates ,
each merge an atom target matches recording the atom emerge would put in the world file, and
each uninstall one matches dropping what then matches nothing.
.SS update
Shows the updates as
.B updates
does, then has
.B emerge \-u \-\-oneshot
merge them, so that nothing it merges joins a set. Nothing is merged, and
.B emerge
is not run, when the plan is refused (exit status 6), when there is nothing to merge, or when the
installed packages' database under the root cannot be written (exit status 1: run
.B egraph
as root). Otherwise the same request goes to
.B emerge \-\-pretend
first, as
.B \-\-verify
sends it, and nothing is merged if its merge list differs (exit status 5). Then
.B egraph
asks, and on yes runs
.B emerge
on the request under the same roots: without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
but for its options that change only how emerge carries out a plan
.RB ( \-\-jobs ", " \-\-load\-average ", " \-\-keep\-going ", " \-\-quiet\-build ", " \-\-quiet\-fail ,
.BR \-\-fail\-clean ", " \-\-buildpkg ", " \-\-buildpkg\-exclude ", " \-\-alert ", " \-\-color ,
.BR \-\-quiet ", " \-\-nospinner ),
read through
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-emerge\-options" .
When the plan replaces old slots
.RB ( replace\-slots ,
under
.BR updates ),
.B emerge \-\-depclean
is then given each as
.BI = cpv
once emerge has merged, with the same options.
Messages the ebuilds logged follow, read from what elog's
.B save_summary
module appended to its log while emerge ran (emerge's own
.B echo
of them is left out then, when the log holds every class it shows). The stores are refreshed
as soon as emerge ends, even when it fails (exit status 1), and what
.B notices
lists follows; run from a terminal without
.BR \-\-yes ,
configuration updates waiting offer
.BR dispatch\-conf .
Run from a terminal, a plan that needs USE changes offers to write them first, as
.B updates
does, and is made again. Without a terminal to ask on, or in the shell, only
.B \-\-yes
merges (exit status 2 otherwise); the interface runs no actions yet.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
.TP
.B \-\-tree
.TP
.B \-\-world
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
As for
.BR updates .
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.SS install \fItarget\fR...
Shows the plan for the targets as
.B plan
does, then has
.B emerge
merge it, as
.B update
does. The targets join
.B @selected
as
.B emerge
adds them, unless
.BR \-\-oneshot .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
As for
.BR plan .
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-oneshot
Add the targets to no set, as
.B emerge \-\-oneshot
does.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.SS exec \fItarget\fR...|\-\-resume
Shows the plan for the targets and verifies it as
.B install
does, then merges it without
.BR emerge :
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-worker" " (" egraph\-build (1))
processes build the packages, as many at once as the jobs allow, and merge them one at a time,
as
.B emerge
schedules them with
.B FEATURES=merge\-wait
(portage's default; without it, a built package merges while builds run, but for those
.B \-\-merge\-wait\-scope
holds): a build starts once nothing it reaches through what each package waits for
is still to merge, a built package merges once no build runs, in the order the builds finished,
and each package a blocker needs gone, or old slot the plan replaces, is uninstalled once the
merges that need it gone are done.
A line is shown for each phase as it starts. With more than one job, what the builds print goes
only to their logs, and a build starts beside others only while
.B PORTAGE_TMPDIR
has the free space
.B emerge \-\-jobs\-tmpdir\-require\-free\-gb
asks (18 GiB by default, and 1 GiB for each build running). Under
.B FEATURES=jobserver\-token
each build takes a token from the jobserver
.B MAKEFLAGS
names, as
.B emerge
does. A package that fails is named with the phase and its build log; nothing new starts after
it, but the builds running finish and what built still merges, and then, with
.BR \-\-keep\-going ,
the run goes on without what needs it. The run publishes its status in the schema
.B emerge
uses under
.BR FEATURES=observability ,
but in its own
.IR ${EPREFIX}/run/egraph/exec\-<pid>.json ,
which the living app's emerge view reads beside emerge's; it writes none of emerge's files. The
run is logged where
.B \-\-log
says: its start, each phase, each package built, merged or uninstalled with its times, each
failure with its build log, each skip, and its end. When the plan merges portage itself into
the running root of a system install, the workers run on a copy of the portage running before
it, removed at the end, as
.B emerge
updating itself does. The targets join
.B @selected
as
.B emerge
adds them, unless
.BR \-\-oneshot .
The run is recorded in
.IR ${EROOT}/var/lib/egraph/exec.json :
its arguments, the packages it has merged, rewritten as each merges, and how it ended, for
.BR \-\-resume .
Also run as
.BR egraph\-exec .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
As for
.BR plan .
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-oneshot
Add the targets to no set, as
.B emerge \-\-oneshot
does.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Merge without asking, and offer no USE changes.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs " \fIN\fR"
Build up to
.I N
packages at once, as
.B emerge \-\-jobs
does; otherwise as many as
.B EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS
says, one by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-keep\-going " [\fBy\fR|\fBn\fR]"
Go on after a failure, once the builds running have finished and merged, as
.B emerge \-\-keep\-going
does: what emerge's resume would drop is skipped, each named with the dependencies it is left
without. A merge goes when one of its dependencies, or a run-time dependency of an installed
package it reaches, is satisfied by nothing left to merge or installed and not replaced, and
what reaches it goes too, unless something installed matches its atom on it. An uninstall goes
with the merges that needed it. When what is left unsatisfied is already installed, the run
stops, as emerge's resume does. Without a value, or with
.BR y ;
otherwise as
.B EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS
says, off by default.
.TP
.BR \-\-merge\-wait\-scope " deep|system|toolchain|none"
The packages that merge alone, once no build runs and after what was let through to merge
before them, whatever
.B FEATURES=merge\-wait
says, as the portage fork's emerge option of the name picks them:
.B deep
@system's packages and what they need at run time,
.B system
@system's alone,
.B toolchain
the few every build runs (bash, coreutils, gcc, binutils, glibc and the like), or
.BR none ;
otherwise as
.B EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS
says,
.B deep
by default. Once one of them merges before what it needs at run time, no build starts beside
another until that has merged, as
.B emerge
holds them.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Carry out the last run's targets and options again, after it failed or was cut short: the plan
is made and verified afresh, then its merges of packages the last run merged, and that are
installed still, are left out, where a target would be merged again otherwise. The
.BR \-\-jobs ,
.B \-\-keep\-going
and
.B \-\-merge\-wait\-scope
given now replace the last run's; targets and the plan's options cannot be given. Nothing is
done when the last run finished.
.TP
.BI \-\-trace " file"
Write to
.I file
a line as each build, merge and uninstall starts and ends: the seconds since the run started,
what happened
.RB ( build\-start ", " built ", " build\-failed ", " merge\-start ", " merged ", "
.BR merge\-failed ", " uninstall\-start ", " uninstalled ", " uninstall\-failed " or " skipped )
and the cpv, separated by tabs.
.SS remove \fIpackage\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove of the installed packages the atoms (or exact cpvs) match: each one nothing else
keeps, as depclean weighs arguments, keeping every installed package they do not match and
leaving
.B @selected
out, since it deselects them. A package that stays is listed with the packages that need it and
the sets that keep it. Then, as
.B update
does, the request goes to
.B emerge \-\-pretend \-\-depclean
first, nothing is removed if it would remove otherwise (exit status 5), and on yes
.B emerge \-\-depclean
removes them and the stores are refreshed.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-\-with\-bdeps " y|n"
Whether build-time dependencies keep packages, as emerge's option (default
.BR y ).
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS select \fIpackage\fR...
Adds the atoms and sets to
.BR @selected ,
as
.B emerge \-\-select \-\-noreplace
does: shows the plan for what is not installed yet as
.B plan \-n
does, then, as
.B update
does, verifies it, asks, and runs
.BR emerge .
emerge decides the atom it records; once the stores are refreshed, the atoms that joined
.B @selected
are listed. After any action, so are the atoms that joined or left it.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS deselect \fIpackage\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-deselect
would remove from the world file, and from
.I world_sets
for a set, as
.B emerge \-\-pretend \-\-deselect
says, followed by the installed packages
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would then remove that it would not remove now. On yes, as
.B update
does,
.B emerge \-\-deselect
removes them; nothing is merged or removed.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Run emerge without asking.
.SS sync \fR[\fIrepository\fR...]
.B emaint sync \-\-auto
syncs the repositories set to auto-sync, as
.B emerge \-\-sync
does, or
.B emaint sync \-\-repo
the repositories named, by name or alias, as
.B emerge \-\-sync
.I repository
does; then the stores are refreshed; then the updates, as
.B updates
shows them with the same options, and the notices, the news the sync brought among them.
Whether the repositories can be written is for
.B emaint
to judge: syncing as a user who cannot fails there. The updates and notices follow a failed
sync too, for the repositories that synced; the exit status is then 1.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
.TP
.B \-\-tree
.TP
.B \-\-world
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
.TP
.B \-\-verify
As for
.BR updates .
.SS notices
What needs attention once
.B emerge
has run, as it reports it: the configuration files under
.B CONFIG_PROTECT
with
.I ._cfg
updates waiting (which portage finds only where the user can write), the repositories'
unread news items with their titles, and the libraries emerge preserved for what still uses
them, with the packages that do, read through
.BR "egraph\-build \-\-notices" ;
and, matched against the stores, the GLSAs affecting an installed package, as
.B glsa\-check \-t affected
finds them: those the repository index holds, for the system's
.BR ARCH ,
less those
.B glsa\-check \-\-inject
marked applied; the repositories synced longer ago than
.B stale_days
allows; the installed packages masked now, with why as emerge words it; and the sonames an
installed package needs that nothing installed provides, less those a preserved library still
does.
As lines, an update is its file,
.BR config
and the update; a news item is the item,
.BR news ,
its repository and its title; a preserved library is its path,
.BR preserved ,
the package it comes from and the packages using it, space-separated; and each atom of
.B @preserved\-rebuild
follows with
.BR rebuild ;
then for each package a GLSA affects, the GLSA's id,
.BR glsa ,
its title, the installed version and the versions that fix it, space-separated; a stale
repository is its name,
.B stale
and when it synced in seconds since the epoch; a masked package is its cpv,
.B masked
and its reasons, comma-separated; a missing soname is the cpv needing it,
.BR missing ,
its multilib category and the soname; and when the last plan
.B watch
made followed a configuration edit alone (the files under the config root's
.I etc/portage
changed, and nothing else the stores read), what it did to the plan:
.BR title ,
.B plan
and how the counts moved ("Configuration edit: +4 rebuilds, \-1 upgrade, now refused"), then
.BR detail ,
.B plan
and each file edited, each line of the plan it gained (+) and each it lost (\-), until the next
plan.
After an action run from a terminal without
.BR \-\-yes ,
preserved libraries have
.B egraph install \-\-oneshot @preserved\-rebuild
planned and offered at once, not again after that rebuild.
.PP
Each notice has a key: a GLSA's
.BI glsa: id\fR,
.BI news: repository / item\fR,
.BR config ,
.BR preserved ,
.BI stale: repository\fR,
.BI masked: cpv\fR,
.BI missing: cpv
and
.BR plan .
A user can set one aside, by its key or what follows the colon; what is set aside is left out
here, after an action (neither its rebuild nor
.B dispatch\-conf
offered), and from
.BR status ,
and kept in
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-~/.local/state}/egraph/set\-aside.json .
.TP
.BI \-\-dismiss " name"
Leave the notice out until what it says changes: a GLSA revised or affecting another version,
a repository synced again, other reasons or libraries.
.TP
.BI \-\-later " name"
Leave the notice out for a while, or until it changes.
.TP
.BR \-\-for " hour|day|week"
How long
.B \-\-later
puts a notice off (default
.BR day ).
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also the notices set aside.
.PP
Setting notices aside writes, as lines, each key and
.BR dismissed ,
or
.B later
and until when in seconds since the epoch.
.SS export \fR[\fIpackage\fR...]
Part of the graph, or all of it without packages, for other tools.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " dot|json"
Graphviz or JSON (default
.BR dot ).
.TP
.B \-\-evaluated
The evaluated store instead, whole and as JSON: the dependencies emerge reads by default, and the
versions each installed package could move to.
.TP
.B \-\-repository
The repository index instead, whole and as JSON: every version in the repositories, and the
configuration that decides which are visible.
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " n"
Dependency edges to follow out from the packages (default 1).
.TP
.BR \-\-direction " reverse|forward|both"
Which edges to follow (default
.BR reverse ).
.SS stats
Store and graph statistics.
.SS log \fR[\fIrun\fR]
The runs logged where
.B \-\-log
writes, read back from the journal (through
.BR journalctl )
or the file: a line each, oldest first, with when it started, its id, the command and its
targets, and how it ended
.RB ( done ", " failed ,
or
.B unfinished
when it was cut short or runs still) with what it merged, uninstalled, failed and skipped and
how long it took. The commands that hand the system to emerge or emaint
.RB ( install ", " update ", " select ", " remove ", " deselect ", " sync )
log a run of two events, its start with the command line run and its end with how the program
exited; their line shows how long it took and why it failed, and their counts are zero. As
lines, the fields are separated by tabs: the id, the start in seconds since the epoch, the
command, the status, the four counts, the seconds (empty when unfinished) and the targets.
.TP
.I run
The events of the run whose id this is, or starts with: each with its time and message, or as
lines, as the file holds them, a JSON object each.
.SS diff \fR[\fIwhen\fR]
The installed packages and root sets now against the system as its history kept it (see
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/lib/egraph
under
.BR FILES ),
by default against the newest generation: what the last change did. Each package upgraded,
downgraded, rebuilt (merged again, or with other flags), new or uninstalled, with its versions and
the flags turned on (+) and off (\-), then the atoms each root set gained and lost. As lines, the
fields are separated by tabs: the cpv before (empty for a new package), the change
.RB ( upgrade ", " downgrade ", " rebuild ", " new ", " uninstall ),
the cpv after (empty for an uninstall) and the flags, separated by spaces; for a root set, the
set, the change
.RB ( added " or " removed )
and the atom.
.TP
.I when
An age
.RB ( 12h ", " 3d ", " 2w )
or a date
.RB ( 2026\-09\-30 ,
from the start of that day, local time): against the system as it was then, from the generation
that held it; nothing changed when no generation ended since. When the history does not reach
back that far, it says since when the changes are. Or a generation's file name.
.TP
.B \-\-json
The changes as a JSON object:
.BR changes ,
a list of objects with the
.BR change ,
the cpvs
.B before
and
.B after
and the flags
.RB ( use )
for a package, or the
.BR change ,
.B set
and
.B atom
for a root set's; the
.B generation
compared against, and
.BR since ,
in seconds since the epoch.
.SS history \fR[\fIwhen\fR] [\fIpackage\fR...]
The system store's history log (see
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/lib/egraph
under
.BR FILES ),
oldest first: each merge, upgrade, downgrade, rebuild and uninstall with its time and versions.
With packages, then, for each the log saw arrive, what pulled it in: its
.B why
chain in the first generation holding it, as the system was just after it merged, or as it is
kept now when no generation holds it any more. As lines, the log's JSON objects as it holds
them, without the chains.
.TP
.I when
An age
.RB ( 12h ", " 3d ", " 2w )
or a date
.RB ( 2026\-09\-30 ,
from the start of that day, local time): only the events since.
.TP
.I package
An installed cpv or a portage atom, matched by name and version against the versions each event
names, before and after: the log holds no slots, repositories or USE.
.SS status
The updates
.B watch
last planned (see
.I status.json
under
.BR FILES ),
read without planning anything: for a status bar. On a terminal, the counts that are not zero,
the notices
.B watch
found (see
.I notices.json
under
.BR FILES ),
each repository's last sync and when the plan was made, or that the stores changed since. As
lines, tab-separated: a key and its value for
.BR upgrades ", " downgrades ", " rebuilds ", " new ", " held ", " uninstalls ", " masked ,
.BR refused " and " current
.RB ( yes " or " no ),
and
.B written
(seconds since the epoch), then
.BR synced ,
the repository and its sync time in seconds for each repository, empty without one, and
.B notices
and how many. Without
.BR \-\-store ,
the status file beside the system store or the user's, whichever is current against its stores,
else the newer.
.TP
.B \-\-json
The status file as JSON, with whether it is
.BR current .
.TP
.B \-\-update
Plan the updates and write the status file first, for a system without the
.B egraphd
service.
.SS tui
The interactive interface: the installed packages, pending updates, orphans and broken ones, each
package's page, the plan, the running emerges, a search through the repositories
.RB ( s ,
as
.BR search ;
.B tab
adds descriptions) whose packages not installed open a listing of their versions, and a
.B :
prompt taking these commands. It keeps the store current while it is open, and loads the
repository index the first time it searches, building it where it is missing or stale.
The list has a page per set its updates are planned for:
.BR @installed ,
.B @world
and
.BR @system ,
turned with the left and right arrows
.RB ( h " and " l );
.B enter
opens a package. A page's plan is made in the background the first time it shows, as
.B exec \-uDN
on its set plans it. Beside them, the plan of
.B @world
the status file holds (see
.BR status ),
counted, with the repository synced longest ago.
It also changes the system:
.B space
picks packages in the list,
.B U
updates them (every update of the page's set with none picked) as
.BR "exec \-\-oneshot \-u \-N" ,
.B r
removes them (every orphan shown with none picked) as
.BR remove ,
and
.B i
installs a search result, or the version under the cursor, as
.BR exec .
Each shows what its command would do and runs it once confirmed, beside the interface, in the
emerge view; the run goes on if the interface quits. Its output goes to
.IR ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/lib/egraph/interface.log ,
and a failed build shows the end of its log.
The bar at the bottom shows the keys for what is selected, and
.B ?
lists every key of the view.
A last page lists the notices egraph watch wrote beside the status file (see
.BR notices ),
its tab counting them, with the selected one's detail below them;
.B x
dismisses it and
.B z
puts it off for an hour, a day or a week, as
.B notices \-\-dismiss
and
.B \-\-later
do.
.B enter
works on it: a GLSA's packages are updated as
.B U
updates them, a package missing libraries is rebuilt as
.BR "exec \-\-oneshot" ,
preserved libraries as
.BR "exec \-\-oneshot @preserved\-rebuild" ,
and a plan a configuration edit changed is the world's updates, as
.B U
on @world with nothing picked,
each shown first as an action is; a stale repository is synced as
.BR sync ,
once asked; a masked package's page opens; a news item is shown, and marked read as it closes,
as
.B eselect news read
does (set aside for the user instead where portage's news lists cannot be written); and for
configuration updates the interface steps aside while
.B dispatch\-conf
(or
.BR \-\-dispatch\-conf )
runs in the terminal, coming back once it exits.
.TP
.B \-\-notices
Open on the notices page, as
.B notify
does.
.SS shell
Commands read one per line from standard input, the stores loaded once; the global options that
choose the stores are taken from the command line only.
.SS rebuild
Rebuild the store from scratch.
.SS refresh
Bring the stores and the repository index up to date if what they were built from changed,
printing nothing; the portage hooks run it after every emerge and sync.
.SS watch
Keep the stores and the repository index fresh until stopped (SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP): the
directories holding what they were built from are watched, and once changes to them have
settled (3 seconds without another, or a minute after the first), they are refreshed as
.B refresh
does. A refresh that fails is logged and tried again on the next change. After a refresh, as
the
.B plan
setting says, the updates are planned as
.B "updates \-\-world \-D \-N \-\-held"
plans them and written to
.I status.json
beside the store; and whatever that setting, when a store changed, the notices are found as
.B notices
finds them and written to
.I notices.json
beside it, each notice new since the last file logged. When the plan follows a configuration
edit alone, what the edit did to it (the files edited, the counts before, the lines gained and
lost) is kept in
.I status.json
too, until the next plan, and is the
.B plan
notice. The
.B egraphd
service runs it as the
.B egraph
user, a member of the
.B portage
group, with only
.IR /var/cache/egraph " and " /var/lib/egraph
writable:
.B systemctl enable \-\-now egraphd
or
.BR "rc\-update add egraphd" .
.SS notify
Keep a desktop notification summing up the notices up, until stopped (SIGTERM, SIGINT or
SIGHUP), through the session bus's notification server: posted when a notice comes that the last
summary did not carry as it is, or one put off comes back, in place of the summary still up, and
closed once nothing is left. Its
.B Open
(or a click on it) starts
.B "tui \-\-notices"
in a terminal: the
.B terminal
setting's, else
.BR xdg\-terminal\-exec ,
else
.BR $TERMINAL ,
else the first found of
.BR foot ", " alacritty ", " kitty ", " konsole ", " gnome\-terminal " and " xterm .
.B Later
puts what it carries off for a day and
.B Dismiss
sets it aside, as the interface does. What the last summary carried is kept in
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-~/.local/state}/egraph/notified.json ,
so a new session does not repeat it. One runs a session: a second exits at once. Installed with
the notify feature, an XDG autostart entry starts it with each desktop session.
.SS check
Compare the store with a fresh build, exiting 4 when they differ.
.SS affected
What a set of merges can affect, as JSON, for portage's neighborhood completion.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BI \-\-request " file"
The JSON request (default
.BR \- :
standard input).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR ROOT ", " PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ", " PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX
Defaults for
.BR \-\-root ", " \-\-config\-root " and " \-\-eprefix .
.TP
.BR EGRAPH_STORE ", " EGRAPH_BUILD ", " EGRAPH_EMERGE ", " EGRAPH_DISPATCH_CONF ", " EGRAPH_EMAINT ,
.BR EGRAPH_LAYOUT ", " EGRAPH_GLYPHS ", " EGRAPH_LOG ", " EGRAPH_LOG_FILE
Defaults for
.BR \-\-store ", " \-\-builder ", " \-\-emerge ", " \-\-dispatch\-conf ", " \-\-emaint ,
.BR \-\-layout ", " \-\-glyphs ", " \-\-log " and " \-\-log\-file .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colour unless
.B \-\-color always
asks for it.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where the user's own store goes (default
.IR ~/.cache ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT}/etc/egraph/replace\-slots
Atoms, one a line
.RB ( #
starts a comment), of the packages whose old slots a plan replaces rather than keeps beside a new
one; none by default, as emerge.
.TP
.I ${PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT}/etc/egraph/egraph.conf
Settings,
.IB key " = " value
a line
.RB ( #
starts a comment):
.RS
.TP
.BI history_days " days"
How long generations of the system store are kept (default 90); 0 keeps none.
.TP
.BR plan " " refresh | sync | never
After which refreshes
.B watch
plans the updates for
.IR status.json :
each (the default), only those after a sync or a configuration edit (the repository index
rebuilt), or none.
.TP
.BI stale_days " days"
After how many days since its sync (its
.IR metadata/timestamp.chk )
a repository is among the notices (default 7); 0 never.
.TP
.BI terminal " command"
The terminal
.B notify
starts the interface in, its words before the interface's command line (as
.BR "wezterm start \-\-" );
unset, one found as
.B notify
describes.
.RE
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/lib/egraph
The system store's history: when a refresh changes the installed packages or the root sets, what
changed is added to
.IR history.log ,
a JSON object a line with the
.BR cpv ,
the
.B event
.RB ( merged ", " upgraded ", " downgraded ", " rebuilt ", " uninstalled ),
the version upgraded or downgraded
.BR from ,
and the
.B time
it merged (when its vdb entry was written) or its uninstall was found; and the store it replaced
is kept as
.IR installed\-<ended>.egraph ,
named by when the system stopped being as it holds (UTC). All generations from the last day are kept, then the newest of
each day, for
.B history_days
days; the log is kept whole.
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/cache/egraph/installed.egraph
The system store, with the evaluated store and the repository index
.RI ( installed.repository.egraph )
beside it; written by root, or by the
.B egraph
user the
.B egraphd
service runs as.
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/cache/egraph/status.json
The updates
.B watch
last planned, beside the store it refreshed: a JSON object with the
.B command
planned
.RB ( "updates \-\-world \-D \-N \-\-held" ),
its output's
.BR lines ,
the
.B counts
of
.BR upgrades ", " downgrades ", " rebuilds ", " new ", " held ", " uninstalls " and " masked
packages and whether emerge
.BR refused
the plan, each repository's
.B name
and the time its snapshot was
.B synced
(from its
.IR metadata/timestamp.chk ,
none without one), the build times of the
.B stores
it was planned from (in nanoseconds), when it was
.BR written ,
and its
.BR format .
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/cache/egraph/notices.json
The notices
.B watch
last found, beside the store it refreshed: a JSON object with the
.B notices
(each its
.B kind
.RB ( glsa ", " missing ", " preserved ", " masked ", " stale ", " config " or " news ),
a
.B key
it keeps while it lasts, a
.BR title ,
.B detail
lines, the installed
.B packages
it is about, the
.B file
of a news item's text, a
.B fingerprint
that changes with what it says, and since when it was noticed, as
.BR since ),
a GLSA, a news item, a stale repository, a masked package and a package's missing libraries
each one notice, the configuration updates and the preserved libraries one each; each
repository's
.B name
and when it
.BR synced ,
from which a reader finds the stale ones again as time passes; the build times of the
.BR stores ,
when it was
.BR written ,
and its
.BR format .
Configuration updates and unread news can change without a refresh;
.B notices
reads them anew, and a news item no longer unread, or a configuration file without a
.I ._cfg
update beside it any more, is left out of
.B status
and the interface.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME}/egraph/installed.egraph
The store of a user who cannot write the system store, used while the system store is not
current.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/postsync.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after a sync.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/post_emerge.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after an emerge that changed the installed packages, once the dispatcher is
linked in place.
.TP
.I /usr/share/egraph/post_emerge
The dispatcher that runs every hook in
.IR /etc/portage/post_emerge.d .
portage runs only
.I /etc/portage/bin/post_emerge
after an emerge, which is the user's own file; without one, link this there.
.TP
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/egraphd.service ", " /etc/init.d/egraphd
The
.B egraphd
service, which runs
.BR "egraph watch" ;
the hooks are not needed while it runs.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as a package that is not installed or a store that could not be built.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 3
The command is not in this build:
.B tui
without Notcurses.
.TP
.B 4
.B check
found the store differing from a fresh build.
.TP
.B 5
.B \-\-verify
found emerge merging otherwise, or an action found it merging or removing otherwise.
.TP
.B 6
emerge would refuse the plan of
.BR updates ", " plan ", " update " or " install :
it has blockers emerge cannot resolve, dependencies nothing satisfies, REQUIRED_USE its USE
leaves unsatisfied, or USE changes it needs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B egraph rdeps dev\-libs/openssl
What depends on openssl, and through which atom.
.TP
.B egraph why net\-misc/networkmanager
Why networkmanager is installed.
.TP
.B egraph updates \-DN \-\-tree
What
.B emerge \-uDN @installed
would merge, under what keeps it.
.TP
.B egraph \-\-layout lines orphans
What depclean would remove, one cpv a line, for a script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR egraph-build (1),
.BR emerge (1),
.BR portage (5)
